#!/bin/bash
# blackmoon-blackarch — prints the steps to add the real BlackArch
# repo to an INSTALLED Black Moon OS system. THIS SCRIPT RUNS
# NOTHING ITSELF: adding a third-party repo changes pacman.conf and
# the keyring, so you read the steps, then run them yourself.
cat <<'EOF'
== Black Moon OS -> BlackArch ==

BlackArch is not a separate distro: it is a large extra repo
(2000+ security tools in ~50 groups) that sits on top of Arch.
Black Moon OS already ships the official-repo toolkit (see
blackmoon-sec); this adds the rest.

Steps (run in a terminal, as root or with sudo):

  1. Get the official installer script:
       curl -O https://blackarch.org/strap.sh
  2. Check it is the one BlackArch publishes (compare the sha256
     sum shown at https://blackarch.org/downloads.html):
       sha256sum strap.sh
  3. Run it (it adds the repo to pacman.conf and the keyring):
       chmod +x strap.sh
       sudo ./strap.sh
  4. Sync and update:
       sudo pacman -Syu
  5. Install one group at a time, e.g.:
       sudo pacman -S blackarch-webapp
       sudo pacman -S blackarch-scanner
       sudo pacman -S blackarch-forensic
     (Full group list: pacman -Sg | grep blackarch)

Honest warnings:
  - Do NOT install the whole "blackarch" group blindly: it is
    2000+ tools and tens of GB. Pick the groups you actually use.
  - This only makes sense on an INSTALLED system, not the live
    ISO session (live changes vanish on reboot).
  - BlackArch tools are for systems you have written permission
    to test. Nothing here changes that rule.
EOF
